      "created_at": "2025-10-20T21:18:16Z",
      "updated_at": "2025-10-20T21:18:16Z",
      "body": "I messed around a bit and found something that should work for all the combinations listed in this thread. I read the keyboard device using regex:\r\n```\r\n$kbdtype = $(brightnessctl -l | grep -Po \"(?<=Device ')[^']*kbd_backlight[^']*(?=')\")\r\n```\r\nThis will match any of the combinations above.\r\n\r\nThis can then be used in the actual command:\r\n```\r\nbindeld = ,XF86KbdBrightnessDown, Keyboard Brightness down, exec, brightnessctl --device=$kbdtype set 10%- && $keysswayosd\r\n```\r\n\r\nI also added the same percentage text that is shown for volume and monitor brightness for good measure (although it's a bit of an abomination):\r\n```\r\n$keysswayosd = $osdclient --custom-progress=$(awk \"BEGIN {v=$(brightnessctl --device=$kbdtype get); if (v==0) print 0; else print v/255}\") --custom-icon=input-keyboard --custom-progress-text=$(awk \"BEGIN {v=$(brightnessctl --device=$kbdtype get); if (v==0) printf \\\"%d%%\\\", 0; else printf \\\"%d%%\\\", v/255*100}\")\r\n```\r\n\r\nI'm sure this could be handled more elegantly, maybe with a one-off script to set `$kbdtype` to a static value?",
      "author_association": "NONE",
