      "body": "> On my 2014 MacBook Air, this setup works for me to give steps at 1%, 25%, 50%, 75% and 100%. I don't think discrete steps of 1% (or 0-255) are appropriate for this function because the human-perceived brightness isn't linear. There isn't much perceived difference between 50%-100% imho, and typically 1% is sufficient (and desirable in low light).\r\n> \r\n> ```\r\n> # bind 2014 MacBook Air keyboard brightness keys\r\n> bind = , XF86KbdBrightnessUp, exec, bash -c 'LED=smc::kbd_backlight; CUR=$(brightnessctl -d $LED g); MAX=$(brightnessctl -d $LED m); STEP=$((MAX/4)); [ $CUR -eq 0 ] && NEW=1 || NEW=$((CUR+STEP)); [ $NEW -gt $MAX ] && NEW=$MAX; brightnessctl -d $LED s $NEW'\r\n> bind = , XF86KbdBrightnessDown, exec, bash -c 'LED=smc::kbd_backlight; CUR=$(brightnessctl -d $LED g); MAX=$(brightnessctl -d $LED m); STEP=$((MAX/4)); if [ $CUR -le $STEP ]; then NEW=0; else NEW=$((CUR-STEP)); fi; brightnessctl -d $LED s $NEW'\r\n> ```\r\n\r\nSame for 2013 Macbook Pro. Thanks.",
